From b946c48cfb3ef3a22ec64985a3d55bfe2b2bd478 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 24bit192khz <118996408+24bit192kHz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:29:35 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] [local] dGPU: hot-plug root port, powered right before
 FSP-S, D3cold for Linux (as upstream series v7)

Root port 1 is marked hot-pluggable, so FSP-S keeps it without a link; the GPU
gets its power in the ramstage right before FSP-S instead of the bootblock, and
dgpu_detect() waits for the link after FSP-S. The port is never disabled, so the
one-time full reset after enabling the GPU (CMOS 0x6d) is gone, and with the GPU
off the port stays as an empty hot-plug slot (the WLAN port keeps 00:1c.6).

Linux puts a hot-plug port into D3 only with _PRW, _S0W and the _DSD property
HotPlugSupportInD3 on it: RP01 gets the three objects next to the power
resource. _ON cuts the power again when power good does not come. The option
list (CFR) declares dgpu_enable as a boolean, like the series.

The power-button override still skips the GPU for one boot: read from the power
state saved in the romstage, as the boot menu request does.

Tested as C58 (same code with debug prints): 10 min load under pciehp, 25 driver
cycles, S3 (30 min), hibernate, 3 cold boots, GPU off and on, all in D3cold at idle.
